INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers") VALUES (460919120103019, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving skimming, scanning, intensive and extensive reading, such as finding a date quickly before reading a technical article in depth. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving reading comprehension and evidence-based answers, such as answering an inference question from a passage about a new campus service. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving english tenses and consistency of time, such as describing a completed lab visit and a result that is still valid. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Analyze a realistic problem involving parts of speech and their functions, such as editing a sentence where a modifier is attached to the wrong word. Present a reasoned approach, justify key decisions, and state an important limitation or verification check."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nSkimming is a rapid read to identify the general idea and structure; scanning searches for a specific detail such as a date or name. Intensive reading examines a short passage carefully for meaning, vocabulary and relationships, while extensive reading builds fluency through longer texts for general understanding. Select the technique to match the purpose, preview headings or key words, and return to the passage for evidence before answering. These methods complement rather than replace one another; a reader often skims first, scans for details and then reads a critical section intensively.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nComprehension requires identifying the central idea, supporting details, sequence, purpose, tone and implied meaning. Direct questions can often be answered from an explicit sentence; inference questions require connecting evidence and background understanding without inventing facts. Read the question carefully, locate relevant lines, distinguish fact from opinion and summarize in one''s own words when appropriate. A correct answer should be supported by the passage, not merely by personal belief. For unfamiliar vocabulary, use surrounding sentences and word structure as clues, then verify the interpretation in context.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nTense and aspect locate an action in time and describe whether it is ongoing, completed or connected to another time. Simple forms express facts or events; continuous forms emphasize duration; perfect forms connect an earlier action with a reference time. Maintain consistent time references unless the meaning requires a shift. For example, a report of a completed visit generally uses past forms for events, while a general conclusion may use present tense. Check time expressions and the intended chronology when choosing a verb form.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nNouns name people, places, things or ideas; pronouns replace noun phrases; verbs express actions or states; adjectives modify nouns; adverbs modify verbs, adjectives or other adverbs. Prepositions express relationships, conjunctions connect units, and interjections express reactions. A word''s part of speech depends on how it functions in the sentence, not only on its spelling. During editing, identify the word''s role and check whether modifiers are placed clearly and whether each clause is complete.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should explain the governing concept, justify the chosen method, check the result and acknowledge a relevant limitation."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

